  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B048",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B030",
    "B053"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks created a clean new regime of quantum-controlled precision measurement: the magic-wavelength concept permits confinement without the usual dominant clock perturbation, and the resulting architecture has been independently realized at extraordinary performance. Katori's originating conceptual contribution and Ye's seminal experimental development provide a focused and coherent attribution. The achievement is mature, reproducible across laboratories, and its fundamental-measurement significance is stronger than a merely incremental improvement in timekeeping.",
    "case_against": "The citation must be confined to the optical-lattice-clock architecture rather than absorb the wider history of optical frequency metrology or all later clock applications. We should also establish whether the scientific distinction from a metrological achievement is framed sufficiently sharply.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that optical lattice clocks are experimentally mature, reproducible across laboratories, and have transformed frequency metrology.",
        "response": "I agree, and regard that independent reproducibility as especially important. I would add that the prize case rests on the physical invention of the magic-wavelength lattice-clock regime, not simply on the present record precision of any individual clock."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth identifies Katori as originator of the lattice-clock concept and Ye as a decisive experimental pioneer, while also placing trapped-ion quantum logic very highly.",
        "response": "Her attribution formulation is persuasive. I retain trapped ions as a serious alternative, but the lattice-clock achievement is presently more cleanly bounded: it connects a defining concept to a widely replicated precision platform without requiring us to delimit the much broader, still evolving enterprise of quantum computation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that circuit quantum electrodynamics is a mature and foundational platform but notes the need to distinguish its scope from adjacent quantum-computing developments.",
        "response": "I continue to regard circuit QED as scientifically outstanding, but his qualification reinforces my concern about its proximity to the 2025 superconducting-circuit recognition. B048 offers a comparably rigorous quantum-control achievement with a clearer separation from that recent prize."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The exact historical boundary between Katori and Ye's central contributions and those of other optical-clock pioneers requires detailed archival review.",
      "Whether the final citation should explicitly foreground fundamental tests as well as precision timekeeping remains to be determined."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
